  "body": {
    "id": "thm:cost-optimal-certificate-feasible",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If the independently specified oracle-instance \\(\\mathsf X_{\\mathrm{phys}}=(x_{\\mathrm{str}},\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{src}})\\) is operationally feasible, then the minimum defining \\(\\operatorname{Opt}_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\) is attained by the lexicographically first canonical singleton-ownership switching certificate \\(E_{\\mathrm{sw}}^{\\star}\\).  It outputs its distinct protocol masks, exact \\(|\\mathrm{LIT}|\\)-bit slice labels, source masks, one \\(\\lceil\\log_2(M+1)\\rceil\\)-bit branch index and one finite concrete restriction transcript per nonempty record, and it satisfies \\(N_{E_{\\mathrm{sw}}^{\\star}}\\le B_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\) under the sparse canonical bound.  Each distinct protocol is queried once and cached, and every issued query bit and returned law-handle, support-index, and support-table bit is charged to \\(N_{\\mathcal O}\\).  The claim is exact optimization relative to \\(\\operatorname{Acc}_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\); it asserts neither a scalar identifying functional nor semantic completeness of CTFIDU\\({}^{+}\\) under the event-local positivity class.",
